  recipeId: robotics
  concept: 'Authored objective: Qualify synchronized joint and Cartesian motion.

    Authored
    requirement: Reject an unsafe target and measure forward/inverse kinematics,
    tracking and shared link evidence through a bounded path.

    General teaching
    principle: Motion validation must consider correct reference, feedback, interlocks
    and authored limits before declaring a movement safe or accurate.

    Only fresh,
    authoritative game evidence can establish that this objective is complete.'
  analogy: A coordinated team needs agreement on position and timing before everyone
    moves.
  diagram: '[Authored setup] -> [Check prerequisites] -> [Observe the game evidence]
    -> [Verify objective]'
  misconception: Motion feedback and a command signal are not interchangeable evidence
    of successful positioning.
  baseline: "Before working on \u2018Qualify synchronized joint and Cartesian motion\u2019,
    which component, reference or condition mentioned in the authored requirement
    should you inspect first? Explain your reasoning."
  prediction: "For \u2018Qualify synchronized joint and Cartesian motion\u2019, what
    result or observation would you expect if the required setup is correct? Distinguish
    a predicted result from a measured result."
  practice: "In Level 120, perform the objective \u2018Qualify synchronized joint
    and Cartesian motion\u2019 in the actual game laboratory. Compare fresh evidence
    against: Reject an unsafe target and measure forward/inverse kinematics, tracking
    and shared link evidence through a bounded path.\nThe mission evaluator decides
    completion; this tutorial does not grade your answer."
